{"step_index":2837,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T13:25:29Z","input_tokens":2181,"cache_read_tokens":100944,"output_tokens":3609,"thinking":"Implement a loading state for the run and test buttons to provide visual feedback during processing. Introduce a brief delay or asynchronous operation to allow the UI to update before displaying results. Enhance error handling to include educational tips for common exceptions like syntax, indentation, name, type, index, and zero division errors. Also, incorporate support for a keyboard shortcut.\n\n\nProblem navigation utilizes calculated `prevId` and `nextId` values based on the current problem ID and total problem count, enabling navigation within the problem set. The breadcrumb structure provides context and navigation to dashboard, problem list, and the current problem's topic and title. These components provide immediate and fluid navigation.\n\n\nBreadcrumb navigation varies across different pages, showing the current location within the DSA learning structure: topic detail, full roadmap, or problem catalog. Examining the settings reveals the default for beginner mode is enabled if the user doesn't have a preference. A toggle button in the header will allow users to switch beginner mode on or off.\n\n\nExamining the provided code, the toggle button updates a setting that controls a \"beginner mode.\" When enabled, the code translates Big-O notation into simplified explanations. Disabling this mode leaves the original Big-O notation unchanged. The function `formatComplexityForBeginner` contains the logic for this translation, mapping common Big-O expressions to plain language.\n\n\nIn beginner mode, the complexity tags within the problem workspace and table are simplified through the `formatComplexityForBeginner` function. This function translates Big-O notation into more accessible plain language descriptions. Metadata is hidden to reduce cognitive load.\n\nThe error formatter is designed to provide specific educational tips for common exceptions. It parses error messages to extract the line number and suggests solutions for syntax, indentation, and other issues.\n\n\nThe error formatter identifies specific error types by pattern matching the error message. It then constructs an informative title and provides a targeted tip for each recognized error, including syntax errors, undefined variables, index errors, key errors, type errors, division by zero, and recursion errors. The line number from the error is extracted to help pinpoint the issue.\n\n\nThe code checks for an empty state in the problem table after applying filters.  Different messages and tips are provided depending on the active filter (bookmarked, review, notes, solved, or search query). If the filter results in no problems, specific guidance is offered to the user to either broaden their criteria or use specific features. This approach tailors the feedback to the user's current context.\n\n\nEmpty filter state guidance is generated to provide context to the user after filtering. CSS gaps and margins are being refined for a more compact and consistent UI layout. Adjustments are being made to padding and margins for various components to improve visual harmony and spacing.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"C:/Users/CVT/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/code-flow-visualizer/app_build/styles.css\"","EndLine":"250","StartLine":"100","toolAction":"\"Viewing visualizer layout in styles.css\"","toolSummary":"\"View visualizer layout styles\""}}]}
